A bundle whose trusted root contains two Fulcio intermediates with the same
Subject DN (CN=sigstore-intermediate,O=sigstore.dev) but different keys should
still verify: the client must resolve the SCT issuer from the cryptographically
verified certificate chain, not by Subject-name lookup.

The signing material is the standard beacon-identity happy-path bundle. The only
change is the custom trusted_root.json, which prepends a decoy certificate
authority whose intermediate shares the genuine intermediate's Subject DN (byte
-for-byte, PrintableString-encoded) but carries an unrelated key. Fulcio's SCT
is verified against issuer_key_hash = SHA-256(issuer SubjectPublicKeyInfo)
(RFC 6962 precert), so a client that picks the issuer by Subject name alone
hashes the decoy's key and fails with an invalid SCT signature — even though the
bundle is genuine. A correct client sources the issuer key from the verified
chain and succeeds.

This mirrors a real incident: during the July 2026 multi-region migration,
Fulcio staging began serving a second, identically-named intermediate, which
broke name-only SCT issuer resolution in at least one client
(sigstore-rust, fixed in https://github.com/sigstore/sigstore-rust/pull/154).

trusted_root.json is generated by make_fixture.py (kept in this directory for
reproducibility); the committed JSON is the canonical fixture.
